1
00:00:00,010 --> 00:00:03,540
Kolberg, a little Baltic
town, sometime in the 1740s.

2
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:06,220
A drum rolls across the market square.

3
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:10,130
A wagon creaks in, piled high
with lumpy brown things.

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:11,380
People sniff them.

5
00:00:11,890 --> 00:00:12,930
Someone tastes one.

6
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:14,790
Then they offer one to a dog.

7
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:17,240
The dog sniffs it — and walks away.

8
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:19,190
Everyone agrees.

9
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:23,270
These things don't smell, don't taste,
and not even the dogs will eat them.

10
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:25,110
Oh, and the wagon?

11
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:27,210
It was a gift from the King.

12
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:31,410
Today, that King lies in a plain
stone tomb in Potsdam, Germany.

13
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:33,860
And people still leave potatoes on it.

14
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:35,990
They're thanking him for a clever trick.

15
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:39,060
So, why was Europe afraid of the potato?

16
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:40,490
And was it, really?

17
00:00:41,290 --> 00:00:46,640
The usual story: scared farmers, tricked
by a clever king and a clever pharmacist.

18
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:49,360
But the old records
tell a different story.

19
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:51,450
And it has six parts.

20
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:53,930
One is a law that nobody can find.

21
00:00:54,550 --> 00:00:56,090
Another starts in a prison camp.

22
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:59,430
And one is a field with guards
who go home every night.

23
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:02,830
Whenever a famous story
shows up, we'll check it.

24
00:01:03,670 --> 00:01:06,010
Myth, disputed, or on the record.

25
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:08,810
Six reasons, six puzzle pieces.

26
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:11,000
Fit them all, and the answer appears.

27
00:01:11,949 --> 00:01:16,039
The potato comes from the Andes,
the high mountains of South America.

28
00:01:16,849 --> 00:01:21,239
In the 1530s, Spanish soldiers
found them stored in village homes.

29
00:01:21,889 --> 00:01:24,439
By the 1560s, potatoes were cargo.

30
00:01:25,149 --> 00:01:29,779
In 1567, barrels of them sailed
from the Canary Islands to Antwerp.

31
00:01:30,259 --> 00:01:32,339
And the shipping record still exists.

32
00:01:33,169 --> 00:01:38,259
In 1588, two of them reached Clusius,
a famous plant expert in Vienna.

33
00:01:38,819 --> 00:01:40,389
A painting of the plant followed.

34
00:01:41,209 --> 00:01:43,289
Look at the name: Taratoufli.

35
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:45,769
That's Italian for little truffles.

36
00:01:46,559 --> 00:01:50,439
In England, John Gerard
printed this picture in 1597.

37
00:01:50,719 --> 00:01:52,899
He called them Potatoes of Virginia.

38
00:01:53,759 --> 00:01:55,339
Wrong part of the Americas, by the way.

39
00:01:55,809 --> 00:01:59,549
Even the name was a mess:
papa, batata, patata.

40
00:02:00,019 --> 00:02:02,549
And newcomers often didn't
know how to plant it.

41
00:02:03,699 --> 00:02:07,900
Back in Kolberg, one grandmother piled
them all in a heap and covered it with a

42
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:08,519
little soil.

43
00:02:09,619 --> 00:02:13,639
Now, famous story number one: Europe
thought potatoes were poison.

44
00:02:14,389 --> 00:02:17,809
They're cousins of deadly
nightshade, a poisonous plant.

45
00:02:18,729 --> 00:02:23,349
The family link is real, but Gerard called
them wholesome food, roasted or boiled.

46
00:02:23,539 --> 00:02:27,949
And historian Rebecca Earle finds that
poison fears weren't what held Europe back.

47
00:02:28,789 --> 00:02:30,759
Piece one clicks in: STRANGER.

48
00:02:31,659 --> 00:02:34,809
A new plant, with a borrowed
name and no instructions.

49
00:02:35,169 --> 00:02:36,619
But strange isn't scary.

50
00:02:37,459 --> 00:02:38,379
So how did it taste?

51
00:02:39,399 --> 00:02:41,079
Let's go back to that dog in Kolberg.

52
00:02:41,449 --> 00:02:42,739
Did that scene really happen?

53
00:02:43,549 --> 00:02:46,149
It comes from the memoir of Joachim
Nettelbeck, a sailor from Kolberg.

54
00:02:46,599 --> 00:02:51,159
He saw it as a small boy and
wrote it down as an old man.

55
00:02:51,759 --> 00:02:53,289
And Kolberg wasn't alone.

56
00:02:54,119 --> 00:02:59,039
In 1765, France's famous Encyclopedia
wrote an entry on the potato.

57
00:02:59,419 --> 00:03:03,139
However you cook it, it said,
the potato is bland and floury.

58
00:03:03,589 --> 00:03:06,089
It can't be counted among pleasant foods.

59
00:03:07,319 --> 00:03:07,829
Ouch.

60
00:03:08,069 --> 00:03:09,099
That's a one-star review.

61
00:03:09,659 --> 00:03:10,279
But read on.

62
00:03:10,869 --> 00:03:16,789
In five French regions, it says, potatoes
were the everyday food of farm families.

63
00:03:17,279 --> 00:03:18,299
For much of the year!

64
00:03:18,659 --> 00:03:20,139
The experts were bored.

65
00:03:20,329 --> 00:03:21,839
Farm families were already eating.

66
00:03:22,179 --> 00:03:24,669
Part of the problem was status.

67
00:03:25,099 --> 00:03:26,899
Potatoes had a name as
filler food for the poor.

68
00:03:27,779 --> 00:03:29,929
So far, nobody sounds afraid.

69
00:03:30,629 --> 00:03:31,179
Bored, yes.

70
00:03:31,689 --> 00:03:32,099
Afraid, no.

71
00:03:32,639 --> 00:03:33,389
Piece two: TASTE.

72
00:03:33,669 --> 00:03:35,589
Not scary, just bland and a bit low-class.

73
00:03:36,369 --> 00:03:38,909
So why are potatoes so hard
to find in old records?

74
00:03:39,349 --> 00:03:40,829
The answer is out in the fields.

75
00:03:41,929 --> 00:03:45,659
Imagine you're a farmer in a
German village around 1700.

76
00:03:46,269 --> 00:03:48,899
Your village farms three huge fields.

77
00:03:49,209 --> 00:03:54,779
Each year, one grows winter grain,
one grows spring grain, and one rests.

78
00:03:55,399 --> 00:03:57,339
That resting field is called fallow.

79
00:03:57,879 --> 00:04:01,449
It gets a year off for the
soil, and the cows graze there.

80
00:04:01,799 --> 00:04:04,399
And the whole village
follows one shared plan.

81
00:04:05,459 --> 00:04:09,589
In one region of northern Germany, about
40 percent of farmland was resting.

82
00:04:10,449 --> 00:04:11,729
So where do potatoes go?

83
00:04:12,579 --> 00:04:14,679
Into the little garden behind the house.

84
00:04:14,769 --> 00:04:15,839
And here's the clever part.

85
00:04:16,639 --> 00:04:19,919
Farmers owed the church a tithe,
one tenth of their harvest.

86
00:04:20,829 --> 00:04:24,779
But a few rows by the kitchen door
were easy to hide and hard to tax.

87
00:04:25,299 --> 00:04:26,979
The tax man barely saw them.

88
00:04:28,079 --> 00:04:30,579
Then potatoes crept into
the resting fields.

89
00:04:30,769 --> 00:04:35,559
And in 1802, church officials in
Anhalt opened a whole file about it.

90
00:04:36,149 --> 00:04:39,349
Its title: the potato tithe
from the fallow field.

91
00:04:39,969 --> 00:04:41,489
Who gets a tenth of a potato?

92
00:04:41,799 --> 00:04:44,169
Historians still argue about the timing.

93
00:04:45,359 --> 00:04:50,379
Earle says farmers grew potatoes in
many parts of Europe by the late 1600s.

94
00:04:51,239 --> 00:04:54,739
Others say big potato fields
only spread after 1800.

95
00:04:55,629 --> 00:04:58,789
Both can be true: gardens
early, fields late.

96
00:05:00,009 --> 00:05:01,629
Piece three: FIELDS.

97
00:05:01,979 --> 00:05:04,059
No room in the plan, so
it hid in the garden.

98
00:05:05,179 --> 00:05:08,619
We're past halfway, and the two most
famous stories are still ahead.

99
00:05:09,159 --> 00:05:11,969
If you like stories checked
against the record, subscribe.

100
00:05:12,989 --> 00:05:15,169
But was the potato actually safe to eat?

101
00:05:16,279 --> 00:05:17,729
Paris, 1771.

102
00:05:18,269 --> 00:05:21,939
France's finance minister sends a
question to the city's top doctors.

103
00:05:22,539 --> 00:05:25,169
Some provinces blame potatoes
for making people sick.

104
00:05:25,649 --> 00:05:26,359
Is that true?

105
00:05:26,849 --> 00:05:29,169
The doctors' answer: potatoes are safe.

106
00:05:29,889 --> 00:05:31,799
So the critics change their story.

107
00:05:32,339 --> 00:05:35,119
Now potatoes harm the grain
and wear out the soil.

108
00:05:36,009 --> 00:05:38,069
Then there's a story
all over the internet.

109
00:05:38,709 --> 00:05:43,429
France banned potatoes in 1748,
over a rumor about leprosy.

110
00:05:44,079 --> 00:05:45,959
Historians have looked for that law.

111
00:05:46,479 --> 00:05:47,459
Nobody has found it.

112
00:05:48,239 --> 00:05:52,859
Earle traces that rumor to one secondhand
note in a plant book from 1620.

113
00:05:54,029 --> 00:05:54,699
Piece four: DOCTORS.

114
00:05:55,189 --> 00:05:58,729
Real doubts, an official
question, and no ban.

115
00:05:59,339 --> 00:06:01,629
Remember the King who sent
that wagon to Kolberg?

116
00:06:02,099 --> 00:06:03,269
He was just getting started.

117
00:06:04,299 --> 00:06:07,169
Prussia, April 1746.

118
00:06:07,639 --> 00:06:12,299
The first royal potato order goes out,
in Pomerania, the region around Kolberg.

119
00:06:12,799 --> 00:06:14,849
It aims to ease a shortage of bread.

120
00:06:15,319 --> 00:06:19,741
Townspeople and farmers should work hard
at growing garden crops, especially

121
00:06:19,741 --> 00:06:20,249
potatoes.

122
00:06:20,689 --> 00:06:24,279
Ten years later, in 1756,
came the most famous one.

123
00:06:24,609 --> 00:06:29,049
Officials must explain why potatoes are
useful, and urge people to plant them that

124
00:06:29,049 --> 00:06:29,419
spring.

125
00:06:30,059 --> 00:06:32,809
How many potato orders
did Prussia send out?

126
00:06:33,149 --> 00:06:34,239
One, five, or fifteen?

127
00:06:34,669 --> 00:06:35,339
Have a guess.

128
00:06:35,939 --> 00:06:36,429
Fifteen.

129
00:06:37,479 --> 00:06:40,639
Historians count at least
fifteen potato orders.

130
00:06:41,309 --> 00:06:44,419
If you need fifteen orders, the
first fourteen didn't quite work.

131
00:06:45,239 --> 00:06:47,829
Now, Germany's favorite potato story.

132
00:06:48,669 --> 00:06:52,479
Frederick plants a royal potato
field and puts soldiers around it.

133
00:06:53,029 --> 00:06:56,279
Farmers think: if the King
guards it, it must be valuable.

134
00:06:56,849 --> 00:06:59,169
So at night, they sneak
in and take some home.

135
00:06:59,799 --> 00:07:00,419
Trick complete.

136
00:07:01,089 --> 00:07:02,309
Potatoes everywhere.

137
00:07:03,109 --> 00:07:07,994
Jürgen Luh, a historian for Prussia's old
palaces, says there's no good evidence for

138
00:07:07,994 --> 00:07:08,199
it.

139
00:07:08,759 --> 00:07:11,599
He adds that Frederick
never ate a potato himself.

140
00:07:12,229 --> 00:07:14,089
Not boiled, not fried.

141
00:07:14,979 --> 00:07:17,829
The hero picture comes partly
from paintings like this one.

142
00:07:18,449 --> 00:07:20,959
It shows Frederick
checking a potato harvest.

143
00:07:21,579 --> 00:07:24,679
The artist painted it a hundred
years after the King died.

144
00:07:25,369 --> 00:07:27,319
So did farmers fight the potato?

145
00:07:27,949 --> 00:07:30,179
Luh says many simply ignored the orders.

146
00:07:30,789 --> 00:07:32,799
Many farmers weren't against potatoes.

147
00:07:32,919 --> 00:07:35,929
They already grew them in
gardens, just not in big fields.

148
00:07:36,529 --> 00:07:37,179
Piece five: ORDERS.

149
00:07:37,779 --> 00:07:42,089
The state wanted potato fields
long before most farmers did.

150
00:07:42,709 --> 00:07:44,959
But orders alone didn't win the argument.

151
00:07:45,349 --> 00:07:46,789
Hungry years did more.

152
00:07:47,669 --> 00:07:49,139
And it starts with a prisoner.

153
00:07:50,279 --> 00:07:54,959
In the 1750s and 1760s, France
and Prussia are at war.

154
00:07:55,569 --> 00:07:58,669
The Prussians capture a
young French army pharmacist.

155
00:07:58,949 --> 00:08:00,919
His name is Antoine Parmentier.

156
00:08:01,159 --> 00:08:04,299
As a prisoner, he's fed
potato mash, day after day.

157
00:08:04,709 --> 00:08:06,659
Later, he'll spend decades promoting it.

158
00:08:07,439 --> 00:08:09,349
Then come the hungry years.

159
00:08:10,229 --> 00:08:15,539
In 1770 and 1771, heavy rain ruins
the harvests in the Czech lands.

160
00:08:15,759 --> 00:08:16,809
Bread gets expensive.

161
00:08:17,099 --> 00:08:18,099
Families go hungry.

162
00:08:18,559 --> 00:08:21,959
In France, an academy in
Besançon runs a contest.

163
00:08:22,759 --> 00:08:25,879
Which plants could stand in
for bread when harvests fail?

164
00:08:26,839 --> 00:08:29,939
Parmentier answers with
the potato, and wins.

165
00:08:30,479 --> 00:08:32,179
Now, the French version of the trick.

166
00:08:32,849 --> 00:08:36,889
Parmentier plants potatoes on a
sandy army field outside Paris.

167
00:08:37,319 --> 00:08:39,919
Guards watch it by day,
and go home at night.

168
00:08:40,739 --> 00:08:43,649
Curious Parisians sneak
in and take the potatoes.

169
00:08:44,069 --> 00:08:44,919
Sound familiar?

170
00:08:45,419 --> 00:08:47,359
It's the same story as Frederick's field.

171
00:08:47,989 --> 00:08:49,369
And it has the same problem.

172
00:08:49,959 --> 00:08:52,879
A colleague named Virey told
it after Parmentier died.

173
00:08:53,599 --> 00:08:56,189
Historians think Virey
borrowed an older tale.

174
00:08:57,059 --> 00:08:59,209
The field itself was real, though.

175
00:08:59,699 --> 00:09:04,629
And in the 1780s, the story goes, he
brings potato flowers to Versailles.

176
00:09:05,209 --> 00:09:06,809
The King pins one to his coat.

177
00:09:07,219 --> 00:09:09,399
Sources don't even agree on the year.

178
00:09:09,939 --> 00:09:12,869
Hungry years made rulers
take the potato seriously.

179
00:09:13,429 --> 00:09:17,609
Officials and scholars started to see
a well-fed people as a strong country.

180
00:09:18,639 --> 00:09:20,079
Piece six: HUNGER.

181
00:09:20,589 --> 00:09:21,659
And there's the full picture.

182
00:09:22,289 --> 00:09:24,239
So, why was Europe afraid of the potato?

183
00:09:24,819 --> 00:09:25,759
Mostly, it wasn't.

184
00:09:26,419 --> 00:09:29,759
Europe wasn't so much afraid of
the potato as unimpressed by it.

185
00:09:30,219 --> 00:09:32,639
And many of the scary
stories were written later.

186
00:09:33,209 --> 00:09:34,769
Remember that dog in Kolberg?

187
00:09:35,189 --> 00:09:36,069
He wasn't scared.

188
00:09:36,509 --> 00:09:37,469
Just unimpressed.

189
00:09:38,099 --> 00:09:40,019
For a long time, neither was Europe.

190
00:09:40,969 --> 00:09:43,879
But once Europe gave it a
real chance, it paid off.

191
00:09:45,139 --> 00:09:46,199
One last guess.

192
00:09:46,639 --> 00:09:52,463
From 1700 to 1900, how much population
growth in Europe, Asia and Africa came from

193
00:09:52,463 --> 00:09:53,069
potatoes?

194
00:09:53,639 --> 00:09:56,529
About five percent, up to
a quarter, or over half?

195
00:09:57,729 --> 00:10:01,259
About a quarter, say economists
Nathan Nunn and Nancy Qian.

196
00:10:01,639 --> 00:10:04,459
A later review puts it
between a sixth and a quarter.

197
00:10:05,689 --> 00:10:07,059
And that tomb in Potsdam?

198
00:10:07,789 --> 00:10:09,389
People still leave potatoes on it.

199
00:10:09,919 --> 00:10:12,779
They're thanking Frederick for a
trick he probably never pulled.

200
00:10:13,409 --> 00:10:16,539
Meanwhile, his fifteen real
orders hardly get a thank-you.

201
00:10:17,259 --> 00:10:19,759
Next time: how did humans invent glass?

202
00:10:20,159 --> 00:10:21,989
Subscribe, and it'll show up for you.

203
00:10:22,919 --> 00:10:27,149
Tell me in the comments: what food
did you hate at first, and love now?

204
00:10:27,819 --> 00:10:28,499
See you next time.
